---
import '../../styles/ports.css'

import { Badge, Code, CodeTabs, Eyebrow, Link } from '@santi020k/lumen-astro'

import ProductAction from '../../components/ProductAction.astro'
import ProductLayout from '../../layouts/ProductLayout.astro'

const description = 'Dark and light Neovim colors with Tree-sitter, LSP diagnostics, completion menus, diffs, and terminal colors from the Santi020k palette.'

const configurations = [
  { value: 'lua', label: 'init.lua', language: 'lua', code: 'vim.opt.termguicolors = true\nvim.opt.background = "dark" -- or "light"\nvim.cmd.colorscheme("santi020k")' },
  { value: 'vim', label: 'init.vim', language: 'vim', code: 'set termguicolors\nset background=dark\ncolorscheme santi020k' }
]
---
<ProductLayout name="Neovim" slug="neovim" title="Santi020k Theme for Neovim — Focus in every buffer" {description}>
  <section class="site-shell port-hero"><div><Eyebrow>For the way you work</Eyebrow><h1>A familiar palette. In every buffer.</h1><p class="lede">Quiet surfaces and clear syntax for your terminal editor. Follow a thought through code, diagnostics, and diffs without losing the thread.</p><div class="port-meta"><Badge variant="outline">Native colorscheme</Badge><span>Neovim 0.10+</span><span>Dark + light</span></div><div class="port-actions"><ProductAction href="/downloads/ports/santi020k-neovim.zip" download>Download Neovim theme</ProductAction><ProductAction href="#install" variant="secondary">Installation guide</ProductAction></div></div><figure class="port-figure"><Code label="init.lua" variant="block" code={'local workspace = {\n  theme = "santi020k",\n  appearance = "dark",\n}\n\n-- A little less noise.\nvim.cmd.colorscheme(workspace.theme)'} language="lua" theme="santi020k" /><figcaption>Code color preview · Neovim uses your own terminal and font</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section id="install" class="site-shell port-section"><Eyebrow>One file to get started</Eyebrow><h2>Keep your configuration. Add the palette.</h2><div class="port-columns"><ol class="port-steps"><li><h3>Find your configuration directory</h3><p>In Neovim, run <code>:echo stdpath('config')</code>. This locates the correct directory on macOS, Linux, and Windows.</p></li><li><h3>Copy the colorscheme</h3><p>Extract the ZIP. Copy <code>colors/santi020k.lua</code> into the <code>colors</code> directory inside your configuration directory, creating it if needed. Keep your other files.</p></li><li><h3>Activate it</h3><p>Add the configuration shown here after your plugin setup, then restart Neovim. Use <code>:set background=light</code> or <code>:set background=dark</code> to change appearance.</p></li></ol><div class="port-code"><h3>Choose your configuration format</h3><CodeTabs items={configurations} ariaLabel="Neovim configuration format" theme="santi020k" /><p>True-color terminal support is recommended. Appearance follows Neovim’s background setting.</p><Link href="/downloads/ports/santi020k.lua" download>Download the Lua file only</Link></div></div></section>
  <section id="coverage" class="site-shell port-section"><Eyebrow>Beyond the main buffer</Eyebrow><h2>The details belong together.</h2><div class="port-columns"><div><h3>Code and editor intelligence</h3><p>Native syntax, Tree-sitter captures, LSP semantic tokens, diagnostic underlines, inlay hints, and references. Tree-sitter and LSP colors apply when those features are configured.</p><h3>Everyday navigation</h3><p>Selections, search, floating windows, completion menus, tabs, diffs, and 16 terminal colors. No runtime plugin dependency.</p></div><div class="port-card"><h3>Update or restore</h3><p>Replace the single Lua file to update. To remove it, choose another colorscheme, remove the Santi020k activation line, and delete the file.</p><p>The first version covers native Neovim groups. Third-party plugins may need their own integrations. Use the ZIP installation; this monorepo’s root is not a plugin-manager entry point.</p></div></div><div class="port-related"><Link href="/terminal/">Match your terminal colors</Link><Link href="/obsidian/">Keep your notes in the same palette</Link></div></section>
</ProductLayout>
